// src/user/input_server/route_kind.pdx -- G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277)
//
// KIND_INPUT_ROUTE = 0x1D7, LINEAR cap derived over KIND_INPUT_SERVER
// (0x1D0). One directed input-event route from a KIND_SEAT's active
// device to a KIND_SURFACE at one moment in focus history. The input
// server (a compositor-INDEPENDENT process; PWP pitfall P2) mints a
// route per focus decision, delivers events through it, and CONSUMES
// it (LINEAR) on focus change. No route, no delivery -- a compromised
// client cannot listen to keystrokes destined for another window
// because it holds no cap to observe them.
//
// Design authority: design/compositor/pwp-spec-vocabulary.md §2.10
// KIND_INPUT_ROUTE (Wave-0 Batch-1 KEYSTONE). Cap-shaped input
// routing is the structural refusal of X11's ambient-broadcast bug
// class (SETXKBMAP / xdotool / keyloggers-as-a-plain-client) and of
// Wayland's implicit-grab stickiness (a grabbed input keeps firing
// after focus moved). One route per focus decision, forecloses both.
//
// PARALLEL LANDING NOTE. B8-01 (G8-M1-001, paideia-os #2276) is
// writing `src/user/input_server/kind.pdx` (or an equivalent) that
// will define KIND_INPUT_SERVER at 0x1D0. This file references
// KIND_INPUT_SERVER as an EXTERNAL numeric slot rather than through
// a module import so ordinal choice is authoritative at that
// landing; if B8-01 rebinds 0x1D0 the parent-slot constant here
// MUST track it and the derived-kinds registry MUST be reconciled
// at batch close. 0x1D0 is currently referenced by
// src/user/compositor/recovery_plane_reserve.pdx as
// RESERVATION_HOLDER_KIND -- the reservation-plane authority IS the
// input server; the two names alias the same process cap kind and
// that aliasing is the whole point of pitfall P8 (input server holds
// the recovery plane so a compositor lockup cannot block recovery).
//
// SLOT ALLOCATION. 0x1D7 is verified free by grep over
// src/kernel/core/cap/*.pdx and src/user/**/*.pdx as of this
// landing (0x1D0 is RESERVATION_HOLDER_KIND / KIND_INPUT_SERVER;
// 0x1D1 is RP_COMPOSITOR_KIND_FORWARD; 0x1D2..0x1D6 are unassigned
// and reserved for B8-01..B8-06 landings; 0x1D8..0x1DF unassigned).
// Main should advance the "Next free derived-kind tag" line in
// design/architecture/next-wave-derived-kinds.md at batch close.
//
// LINEARITY DISCIPLINE (KEYSTONE P2/P10 mitigation).
//
//   * NO R_MINT bit is declared on this kind. A KIND_INPUT_ROUTE is
//     minted BY the KIND_INPUT_SERVER authority (that parent holds
//     R_MINT on this derived family) and never by a client. Once
//     minted, the cap is held by exactly one recipient (the target
//     surface's client) at exactly one moment; the input server's
//     cap-table dispatch enforces the no-clone contract.
//
//   * Consume-on-focus-change contract: SEQ_REVOKE atomically stamps
//     the `revoked` bit in the row header, snapshots the final
//     event_count for audit, and deducts the row from the pool
//     (row goes back to the free list). ONE REVOKE PER MINT. A
//     second revoke attempt against an already-revoked row returns
//     ROUTE_ALREADY_REVOKED; a deliver op against a revoked row
//     returns ROUTE_REVOKED. The row is not reusable -- the next
//     focus decision requires a fresh mint against
//     KIND_INPUT_SERVER.
//
//   * The three deliver rights (DELIVER_KEY, DELIVER_POINTER,
//     DELIVER_TOUCH) mutate the event_count IN PLACE on the row
//     and forward the event payload to the target surface's
//     endpoint; they do not clone the cap and do not defer their
//     edit. Delivery is serialised by the input server's own
//     per-seat event loop, so no cross-thread fencing is needed
//     beyond the atomic revoke stamp.
//
//   * The input server is the ONLY authority allowed to observe or
//     revoke a KIND_INPUT_ROUTE row: R_QUERY_TARGET is
//     server-held (target introspection for debugging), R_REVOKE is
//     server-held (focus-change consumer). A recipient that leaks
//     its cap by process death is cleaned up by the server-side
//     revoke path (a cap held by a dead recipient is recovered
//     when its cap table is torn down; the row's revoked bit stays
//     0 and the row goes back to the free list at torndown time).
//
// FINGERPRINT. One legacy-suffixed mint fingerprint per the standard
// klog convention (matches kind_tui_canvas / kind_tls_trust /
// surface_commit precedent). Emitted once on successful mint by the
// future substrate; no fingerprint on the deliver, revoke, or
// query paths.
//
// LABEL PREFIX: `route_kind_` throughout, sidestepping paideia-as
// reserved-word collisions (`loop`, `if`, `let`, `write`, ...).
//
// MECE SCOPE. Single file under src/user/input_server/. No mod.pdx
// exists in this directory yet (this is the first landing in the
// directory alongside B8-01); the registry marker
// `; wave0-b8-07: route_kind` marks this module's slot if main
// decides to add one. This landing touches no other file.
//
// FAILURE-BAND DEVIATION. The row-spec pinned 0xFFFFEB60..0xFFFFEB6F
// but that band is already taken by KIND_HOTPLUG_CHANNEL (see
// src/kernel/core/cap/kind_hotplug_channel.pdx §3). This landing
// uses 0xFFFFEB70..0xFFFFEB7F instead -- verified free by grep
// over src/**/*.pdx + tests/**/*.pdx as of this landing. Main
// should update the row-spec at batch close to reflect the actual
// band, or reissue with band 0xFFFFEB80..0xFFFFEB8F if collision
// with a parallel batch-8 landing forces a rebind.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1 -- KIND ORDINAL + PARENT
// =============================================================================
//
// The parent-slot constant is a FORWARD REFERENCE to the ordinal
// KIND_INPUT_SERVER occupies after B8-01 lands. Kept as a bare
// numeric literal here to sidestep a module-order dependency in the
// encoder's linker step; the coordination point is the derived-kinds
// registry, which lists both ordinals atomically at batch close.

module RouteKind = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.1 -- KIND ORDINAL + LAM-HINT BASE
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_INPUT_ROUTE       : u64 = 0x1D7
  pub let KIND_INPUT_ROUTE_BASE  : u64 = 0x1D0   // = KIND_INPUT_SERVER (B8-01)

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.2 -- RIGHTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Sparse bit-map so the future dispatcher can `test rax, R_*` and
  // branch on a single bit. CRITICALLY: there is NO R_MINT bit.
  // A LINEAR cap does not carry mint rights; the derivation authority
  // (KIND_INPUT_SERVER) is the sole minter and the input server's
  // per-seat focus-decision gate supplies the mint.
  //
  //   R_DELIVER_KEY      -- forward one key event, bump event_count
  //   R_DELIVER_POINTER  -- forward one pointer event, bump event_count
  //   R_DELIVER_TOUCH    -- forward one touch event, bump event_count
  //   R_QUERY_TARGET     -- read-only: return surface_kind_id (server-held)
  //   R_REVOKE           -- LINEAR consume at focus change (server-held)
  //
  // R_ALL is the OR of every declared bit and is the exact right-set
  // the KIND_INPUT_SERVER mint gate stamps at derivation time. The
  // recipient client typically holds the three deliver bits only;
  // R_QUERY_TARGET + R_REVOKE stay with the input server.

  pub let R_ROUTE_DELIVER_KEY     : u64 = 0x001
  pub let R_ROUTE_DELIVER_POINTER : u64 = 0x002
  pub let R_ROUTE_DELIVER_TOUCH   : u64 = 0x004
  pub let R_ROUTE_QUERY_TARGET    : u64 = 0x008
  pub let R_ROUTE_REVOKE          : u64 = 0x010
  pub let R_ROUTE_ALL             : u64 = 0x01F

  // The client-visible right-set at mint time: three deliver bits.
  // Note: LINEAR means the "client" here is the input server passing
  // the cap forward one hop to the target recipient; the recipient's
  // cap-table entry gets R_ROUTE_CLIENT and the server keeps its own
  // reference with R_QUERY_TARGET | R_REVOKE for the audit + focus-
  // change paths.
  pub let R_ROUTE_CLIENT          : u64 = 0x007

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.3 -- ROW LAYOUT (InputRouteFields, 64 bytes)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // One cache line per row (surface_commit / kind_tui_canvas
  // precedent). The five spec-named fields (seat_kind_id,
  // surface_kind_id, window_kind_id, route_established_seq,
  // event_count) map onto the row with two u32s packed into the same
  // u64 slot (seat + surface) so the row header + three u64s + one
  // packed u64 fill a natural 40-byte prefix; the remaining 24 bytes
  // are reserved for future audit fields (per-key latency histogram,
  // last-event-serial, etc.).
  //
  // Row layout (offsets in bytes):
  //
  //   [+0]  header:
  //             in_use[63:56]           1 iff row is allocated
  //             revoked[55:55]          1 iff SEQ_REVOKE has run
  //                                     (linear-consume gate)
  //             reserved[54:0]          0 at mint; future flags land here
  //
  //   [+8]  route_id: u64, server-assigned monotone token, never 0
  //                   (used by audit journal to correlate delivery
  //                   events against the mint that authorised them)
  //
  //   [+16] seat_and_surface_pack:
  //             seat_kind_id[63:32]     KIND_SEAT (0x1D6, B8-06) cap
  //                                     slot the route was minted from
  //             surface_kind_id[31:0]   KIND_SURFACE (0x1B8) cap slot
  //                                     the route delivers to
  //
  //   [+24] window_kind_id: u64, KIND_WINDOW (0x1C1) cap slot of the
  //                          surface's parent window, cached at mint
  //                          time for fast lookup during delivery
  //                          (surface -> window walk is otherwise a
  //                          layer_tree probe per commit). Zero iff
  //                          the surface is not attached to a window
  //                          (subsurface-only route; delivery still
  //                          works, window_kind_id is diagnostic).
  //
  //   [+32] route_established_seq: u64, monotonic server sequence at
  //                                mint time (from
  //                                _route_kind_established_counter).
  //                                Never 0. Delivery order across
  //                                routes on the same seat is
  //                                totally ordered by this value.
  //
  //   [+40] event_count: u64, count of events delivered on this route
  //                       (sum of key + pointer + touch bumps). Read
  //                       at revoke time and returned to the audit
  //                       journal so a focus-change record carries
  //                       the exact event budget the previous focus
  //                       received.
  //
  //   [+48] reserved0: u64, 0 at mint; future
  //                    per-event-class breakdown lands here.
  //
  //   [+56] reserved1: u64, 0 at mint; future last-event-serial
  //                    lands here.
  //
  // The row is written by exactly one server thread at a time (the
  // per-seat input dispatch is single-threaded per spec); no cache-
  // coherence fencing beyond the atomic revoke stamp is needed.

  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ROW_BYTES   : u64 = 64
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MAX         : u64 = 8    // pool ceiling (surface_commit precedent)

  // Offsets into the 64-byte row.
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_HEADER            : u64 = 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_ROUTE_ID          : u64 = 8
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_SEAT_SURFACE      : u64 = 16
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_WINDOW_KIND_ID    : u64 = 24
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_ESTABLISHED_SEQ   : u64 = 32
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_EVENT_COUNT       : u64 = 40
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_RESERVED0         : u64 = 48
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OFF_RESERVED1         : u64 = 56

  // Header bit positions (in the top byte of the [+0] word).
  //   in_use  = bit 56 of the header word (top-of-byte low bit)
  //   revoked = bit 55 of the header word (top-byte-adjacent)
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_HDR_IN_USE_BIT  : u64 = 56
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_HDR_REVOKED_BIT : u64 = 55

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.4 -- OPS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every op is arity ONE (row_id in target_ptr, sub-op in op_arg[7:0];
  // the deliver ops carry an event payload pointer in op_arg[63:8]).
  // Dispatch lands in a future server-side handler milestone.
  // SEQ_REVOKE is the LINEAR consume: its handler MUST test-and-set
  // the revoked bit atomically and MUST move the row to the free list
  // before returning.
  //
  //   DELIVER_KEY      payload: KIND_KEY_EVENT slot
  //   DELIVER_POINTER  payload: KIND_POINTER_EVENT slot
  //   DELIVER_TOUCH    payload: KIND_TOUCH_EVENT slot
  //   QUERY_TARGET     payload: none; returns surface_kind_id in rax
  //   SEQ_REVOKE       payload: none; consumes the cap, returns
  //                    final event_count in rax for audit

  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_DELIVER_KEY     : u64 = 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_DELIVER_POINTER : u64 = 1
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_DELIVER_TOUCH   : u64 = 2
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_QUERY_TARGET    : u64 = 3
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_SEQ_REVOKE      : u64 = 4
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OP_MAX             : u64 = 4

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.5 -- FAILURE TAXONOMY
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // A fresh 16-wide band: 0xFFFFEB70..0xFFFFEB7F, disjoint from every
  // documented kind band (KIND_HOTPLUG_CHANNEL 0xFFFFEB60..6F,
  // KIND_PAGE_FLIP 0xFFFFEB50..5F, KIND_FRAMEBUFFER 0xFFFFEB40..4F,
  // KIND_TONEMAP_LUT 0xFFFFEB30..3F, KIND_NIC 0xFFFFEB28..2F,
  // KIND_TLS_TRUST 0xFFFFEB20..27, KIND_TUI_CANVAS 0xFFFFEB10..1F,
  // KIND_SURFACE_COMMIT 0xFFFFEA60..6F). 0xFFFFEB70..0xFFFFEB7F is
  // verified free by grep over src/**/*.pdx + tests/**/*.pdx as of
  // this landing. See header note on the deviation from the task-
  // pinned band.

  pub let ROUTE_KIND_OK                 : u64 = 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_TAIL_ENOSPC        : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7F
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_TAIL_BAD_ARG       : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7E
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_PARENT    : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7D
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS    : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7C
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SEAT      : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7B  // seat == 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SURFACE   : u64 = 0xFFFFEB7A  // surface == 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_MINT_SEAT_LOCKED   : u64 = 0xFFFFEB79  // seat lock held
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT           : u64 = 0xFFFFEB78
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_WRONG_KIND         : u64 = 0xFFFFEB77
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_BAD_RIGHTS         : u64 = 0xFFFFEB76
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_BAD_EVENT          : u64 = 0xFFFFEB75  // event payload malformed
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_REVOKED            : u64 = 0xFFFFEB74  // deliver against revoked
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ALREADY_REVOKED    : u64 = 0xFFFFEB73  // double-revoke (linear violation)
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_STALE              : u64 = 0xFFFFEB72  // query after revoke
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_WINDOW_UNKNOWN     : u64 = 0xFFFFEB71  // window walk found no parent
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_REVOKE_ALREADY     : u64 = 0xFFFFEB70
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_DECODE_BAD         : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.6 -- STATS COUNTER SLOTS
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // The server-side substrate will publish a _route_kind_stats
  // [u64; 8] table; slot indices are named here so cross-module
  // bumpers agree. REVOKES vs MINTS lets a boot-log observer verify
  // the LINEAR invariant (MINTS >= REVOKES; deficit == in-flight
  // routes + recipient-death recoveries).

  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_MINTS       : u64 = 0
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_REVOKES     : u64 = 1
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_KEYS        : u64 = 2
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_POINTERS    : u64 = 3
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_TOUCHES     : u64 = 4
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_REFUSED     : u64 = 5
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_DOUBLE      : u64 = 6   // ALREADY_REVOKED occurrences
  pub let ROUTE_KIND_ST_MAX         : u64 = 8

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 1.7 -- FINGERPRINT (one line, legacy-suffixed)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // pdx_kind_input_route_meta: emitted once on successful mint by
  // the future substrate. The legacy-suffix convention is what
  // tools/verify-fingerprint-coverage.sh enumerates, so this literal
  // is the machine-checkable name of the milestone. No fingerprint
  // on the deliver, revoke, or query paths.
  pub let pdx_kind_input_route_meta : [u8; 50] = "input route mint ok [legacy: INPUT ROUTE MINT OK]\0"

  // Short field-name string constant for future debug-print use
  // (mirrors surface_commit_k_commit_id precedent).
  pub let route_kind_k_route_id     : [u8; 9] = "route_id\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 2 -- PACKERS + VALIDATORS (schema-only, pure leaves)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // Every function in this section is a pure, unsafe-bodied leaf with
  // small immediates (no MOVABS r64, imm64 anywhere), returns its
  // result in rax, and clobbers only rax + one scratch register.
  // Encoder-conservative pattern per surface_commit / kind_tls_trust.

  // route_kind_op_valid(op) -> 1 iff op in [0, OP_MAX=4].
  pub let route_kind_op_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (op: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): dispatch op validator. Admits 0..4 (DELIVER_KEY, DELIVER_POINTER, DELIVER_TOUCH, QUERY_TARGET, SEQ_REVOKE). Small immediate. Labels prefixed route_kind_ov_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 4;
        ja  route_kind_ov_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
      route_kind_ov_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // route_kind_seat_surface_pack(seat_kind_id, surface_kind_id)
  //   -> seat_kind_id[63:32] | surface_kind_id[31:0].
  // Both halves masked to 32 bits via shl+shr so an out-of-range
  // argument cannot smear into a neighbour field. Row-write helper
  // for the mint path at [+16]. Encoder-conservative: no
  // `and r11, imm64` -- the 32-bit mask is built by shl 32 / shr 32.
  pub let route_kind_seat_surface_pack : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (seat_kind_id: u64) (surface_kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): pack seat_kind_id[63:32] | surface_kind_id[31:0] per row layout at [+16]. Encoder-conservative build of the 32-bit mask via shl 32 / shr 32 to avoid MOVABS r64, 0xFFFFFFFF. surface_kind_id is masked first (into rax), then seat_kind_id shifted into place and ORed in. Labels prefixed route_kind_sp_ (none needed, straight-line).",
      block: {
        // rax = surface_kind_id & 0xFFFFFFFF (via shl 32 / shr 32)
        mov rax, rsi;
        shl rax, 32;
        shr rax, 32;

        // r10 = seat_kind_id, shifted left 32 (drops any bits above
        // its low 32 -- the intended masking behaviour).
        mov r10, rdi;
        shl r10, 32;

        or  rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // route_kind_hdr_pack() -> canonical row header at mint time.
  // Only the substrate's tail_alloc sets in_use (bit 56); revoked
  // (bit 55) stays 0 until SEQ_REVOKE fires. Mirror of
  // surface_commit_hdr_pack.
  pub let route_kind_hdr_pack : () -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): canonical row header at mint time. Only in_use written by tail_alloc; revoked stays 0 until SEQ_REVOKE fires. Mirror of surface_commit.pdx surface_commit_hdr_pack. Labels prefixed route_kind_hp_ (none needed, straight-line).",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // route_kind_hdr_revoked_set(header) -> header with revoked bit
  // (bit 55) set. Small immediate (55 fits in imm8 as a shift count).
  pub let route_kind_hdr_revoked_set : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (header: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): set revoked bit (bit 55) in the header word. Small-immediate shift by 55 via mov cl / shl. Labels prefixed route_kind_hr_ (none needed, straight-line).",
      block: {
        mov r10, 1;
        mov rcx, 55;
        shl r10, cl;
        mov rax, rdi;
        or  rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 3 -- FACTORY (input_route_mint) -- LINEAR mint gate
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // input_route_mint(seat_kind_id, surface_kind_id) -> route_id (never
  // 0) on success; a ROUTE_KIND_MINT_* sentinel on refusal. This is
  // the STUB gate that establishes the linearity contract for the
  // future server-side handler (G8-M2-004). The gate MUST:
  //
  //   1. Refuse seat_kind_id == 0 with ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SEAT.
  //   2. Refuse surface_kind_id == 0 with ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SURFACE.
  //   3. Refuse if the seat's lock byte is nonzero (the real handler
  //      probes _seat_lock_byte at the seat_kind_id offset; this stub
  //      returns SEAT_LOCKED whenever the caller signals a locked
  //      seat by passing surface_kind_id == seat_kind_id, a synthetic
  //      shape the future dispatcher never uses -- the stub is a
  //      well-formed refusal, not a real lock probe).
  //   4. Walk up surface -> window (a real handler reads
  //      _surface_parent_window at the surface_kind_id row); the stub
  //      returns 0 (window_kind_id unknown, non-fatal) so downstream
  //      harnesses observe a well-formed mint sentinel rather than a
  //      fabricated success.
  //
  // Until the full handler lands at G8-M2-004, this factory always
  // returns 1 (route_id sentinel) after the three refusal checks
  // above pass, without touching any real row storage. Downstream
  // deliver + revoke stubs interpret the returned 1 as "row 0 in
  // the future pool" and refuse deliver against a stale route.

  pub let input_route_mint : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (seat_kind_id: u64) (surface_kind_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): LINEAR mint gate stub. Order: (1) seat != 0 else ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SEAT (0xFFFFEB7B); (2) surface != 0 else ROUTE_KIND_MINT_BAD_SURFACE (0xFFFFEB7A); (3) synthetic-lock check: surface == seat means SEAT_LOCKED (0xFFFFEB79) -- the future handler probes a real per-seat lock byte; the stub uses this equality as a well-formed refusal path. (4) success returns sentinel route_id 1 (never 0); the full row-write path lands at G8-M2-004. No MOVABS anywhere; all sentinels are 32-bit-imm sign-extended. Labels prefixed route_kind_m_.",
      block: {
        // Step 1: seat != 0
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_m_bad_seat;

        // Step 2: surface != 0
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je   route_kind_m_bad_surface;

        // Step 3: synthetic seat-locked probe (surface == seat -> LOCKED).
        // The real handler reads a per-seat lock byte; this stub uses
        // the equality shape so downstream tests can exercise the
        // refusal path without needing a seat-lock table wired up.
        cmp  rdi, rsi;
        je   route_kind_m_seat_locked;

        // Step 4: success -- return route_id sentinel 1.
        mov  rax, 1;
        ret;

      route_kind_m_bad_seat:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB7B;
        ret;

      route_kind_m_bad_surface:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB7A;
        ret;

      route_kind_m_seat_locked:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB79;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 4 -- DELIVER PATHS (bump event_count; forward to surface)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // input_route_deliver_key(route_id, key_event_slot) -> u64
  // input_route_deliver_pointer(route_id, ptr_event_slot) -> u64
  // input_route_deliver_touch(route_id, touch_event_slot) -> u64
  //
  // Each returns:
  //   ROUTE_KIND_OK (0)               on successful bump + forward
  //   ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT             on route_id == 0
  //   ROUTE_KIND_BAD_EVENT            on event_slot == 0
  //   ROUTE_KIND_REVOKED              (future) on revoked-row deliver
  //
  // The real handlers (G8-M2-004) will atomically bump event_count at
  // [row + ROUTE_KIND_OFF_EVENT_COUNT] and forward the event payload
  // to the target surface's endpoint. Until that landing, these stubs
  // enforce the arg-nonzero contract so downstream test harnesses see
  // well-formed refusals rather than fabricated forwards.

  pub let input_route_deliver_key : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (route_id: u64) (key_event_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): deliver-key stub. Returns ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT (0xFFFFEB78) if route_id == 0, ROUTE_KIND_BAD_EVENT (0xFFFFEB75) if key_event_slot == 0, ROUTE_KIND_OK (0) otherwise. Real bump + forward lands at G8-M2-004. Labels prefixed route_kind_dk_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dk_bad_slot;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dk_bad_event;
        xor  rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_kind_dk_bad_slot:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB78;
        ret;
      route_kind_dk_bad_event:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB75;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let input_route_deliver_pointer : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (route_id: u64) (ptr_event_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): deliver-pointer stub. Same shape as deliver_key: BAD_SLOT / BAD_EVENT / OK. Labels prefixed route_kind_dp_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dp_bad_slot;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dp_bad_event;
        xor  rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_kind_dp_bad_slot:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB78;
        ret;
      route_kind_dp_bad_event:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB75;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let input_route_deliver_touch : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (route_id: u64) (touch_event_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): deliver-touch stub. Same shape as deliver_key: BAD_SLOT / BAD_EVENT / OK. Labels prefixed route_kind_dt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dt_bad_slot;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je   route_kind_dt_bad_event;
        xor  rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_kind_dt_bad_slot:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB78;
        ret;
      route_kind_dt_bad_event:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB75;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // SECTION 5 -- QUERY + REVOKE (LINEAR consume)
  // ==========================================================================
  //
  // input_route_target(route_id) -> surface_kind_id (low 32 in rax on
  // success; ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT on route_id == 0). Real handler
  // reads seat_and_surface_pack[31:0] at
  // [row + ROUTE_KIND_OFF_SEAT_SURFACE]. Until G8-M2-004 lands, the
  // stub returns 0 (unknown target) on nonzero route_id so downstream
  // harnesses see a well-formed sentinel.
  pub let input_route_target : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (route_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): target-query stub. Returns ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT (0xFFFFEB78) if route_id == 0, else 0 (unknown target sentinel; real handler reads seat_and_surface_pack[31:0]). Labels prefixed route_kind_tq_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_tq_bad_slot;
        xor  rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_kind_tq_bad_slot:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB78;
        ret
      }
    }

  // input_route_revoke(route_id) -> final event_count (audit value)
  // on success; ROUTE_KIND_ALREADY_REVOKED on double-revoke (future
  // handler enforces the CAS on the revoked bit); ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT
  // on route_id == 0. This is the LINEAR CONSUME point -- one revoke
  // per mint, the cap is dead after this returns.
  //
  // The real SEQ_REVOKE handler (G8-M2-004) MUST:
  //   1. cmpxchg the revoked bit from 0 to 1 in the header word; on
  //      failure return ROUTE_KIND_ALREADY_REVOKED (linear-consume
  //      enforcement point).
  //   2. read event_count from [row + ROUTE_KIND_OFF_EVENT_COUNT].
  //   3. deduct the row from the pool (in_use bit cleared).
  //   4. bump ROUTE_KIND_ST_REVOKES.
  //   5. emit an audit-journal record carrying (route_id,
  //      seat_kind_id, surface_kind_id, event_count) for the P2
  //      auditability discipline.
  //
  // Until that handler lands, this stub returns 0 (event_count
  // sentinel; no events delivered) after refusing the nonzero-arg
  // gate so downstream tests observe a well-formed refusal.
  pub let input_route_revoke : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (route_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "G8-M2-001 (paideia-os #2277): SEQ_REVOKE stub. LINEAR CONSUME point. Returns ROUTE_KIND_BAD_SLOT (0xFFFFEB78) on route_id == 0, else 0 (event_count sentinel; real handler reads the row's event_count and CASes the revoked bit). Does NOT stamp the revoked bit -- a fabricated revoke would defeat the linear-consume invariant this file exists to establish. Labels prefixed route_kind_rv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je   route_kind_rv_bad_slot;
        xor  rax, rax;
        ret;
      route_kind_rv_bad_slot:
        mov  rax, 0xFFFFEB78;
        ret
      }
    }
}
